\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Conjugation signs on a simple module}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{For a nonzero simple complex Hermitian module on #{n\ge1}
positive Euclidean directions, the possible
\vocabk{charge conjugation signs}{fdip-017H} and their squares
depend only on the residue of #{n} modulo #{8}. A plus or minus
entry gives the sign multiplying #{\bbI_S} in #{c_\eta^2}.
A dot means the sign #{\eta} is unavailable on that simple module.
##{\begin{array}{c|cc}
n\bmod 8 & \eta=+1 & \eta=-1\\ \hline
0 & + & +\\
1 & + & \cdot\\
2 & + & -\\
3 & \cdot & -\\
4 & - & -\\
5 & - & \cdot\\
6 & - & +\\
7 & \cdot & +
\end{array}}}

\proof{
\p{On the tensor model in \ref{fdip-017M} let #{\kappa_0}
be conjugation in the displayed basis. The odd-index directions
are real and the even-index ones are purely imaginary. With each product
in increasing order set
##{\begin{aligned}
c_{\mathrm{im}}&=(\varepsilon_2\varepsilon_4\cdots\varepsilon_{2m})\kappa_0,\\
c_{\mathrm{re}}&=(\varepsilon_1\varepsilon_3\cdots\varepsilon_{2m-1})\kappa_0.
\end{aligned}}
The empty product is the identity.
Both maps are antiunitary. Conjugating a generator by a product
of #{m} distinct directions gives sign #{(-1)^{m-1}} if it
belongs to the product and #{(-1)^m} otherwise. Coordinate
conjugation itself negates the imaginary generators only.
The two maps therefore have respective uniform signs
#{(-1)^m} and #{(-1)^{m+1}} on the first #{2m} directions.}

\p{The square of either linear product is #{(-1)^{m(m-1)/2}}
times the identity by reordering. Moving #{\kappa_0} past the
imaginary product gives another #{(-1)^m}, while the real
product is fixed. Thus
##{c_{\mathrm{im}}^2=(-1)^{m(m+1)/2}\bbI,\qquad
c_{\mathrm{re}}^2=(-1)^{m(m-1)/2}\bbI.}
Their even-dimensional signs exhaust the choices. For a given
sign the ratio of any two antiunitaries, one composed with
the other's inverse, is a unitary linear endomorphism commuting
with all directions. It is a unit scalar on a simple by the
argument of \ref{fdip-017J}. Scaling an antiunitary by a unit
scalar leaves its square unchanged.}

\p{For odd dimension, use #{\varepsilon_{2m+1}=\sigma C_{2m}}
from \ref{fdip-017N}. Conjugating the phase #{\ui^m} changes
it by #{(-1)^m}, and the #{2m} direction signs multiply to
#{1}. Thus every candidate acts on the last direction by
#{(-1)^m}, which must equal the requested #{\eta}.
The imaginary-product map has that sign. At #{m=0} it is
coordinate conjugation on a scalar line, and the last operator
is already a real scalar. The square is the same for both
simple types. The signs of the two squares repeat under
#{m\mapsto m+4}, giving the displayed residues.}
}
